import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Settings",
	description: "Settings on the Roc Solid server platform: environment variables (Env.var!), the app's public URL (Env.public_url!), the app's context made once at start, and Env.dev! for what only a developer's machine should do.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "env", label: "Settings: Env" },
		{ id: "context", label: "Read once: the app's context" },
		{ id: "dev", label: "Only while developing" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · settings"
				title="Settings from the environment"
			>
				Secrets, and whatever differs between machines, come from environment variables, as in a
				twelve-factor app. This page covers reading them, the app's public URL, and doing something only on
				a developer's machine. Using secrets (signing links, sealing data) is on the
				<a href={Paths.platform_crypto}>crypto</a> page.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="env">Settings: Env</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_settings.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_settings.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>Env.var!(name)</code> reads a server environment variable: <code>Ok(value)</code> or
					<code>Err(NotFound)</code>. It works the same in a request, a job or a schedule, so read a secret
					where you need it.
				</li>
				<li>
					The file <code>--env-file</code> names is read into the server's environment at start, before
					anything else: <code>Env.var!</code> and every plugin see its
					<code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-…</code> as if the shell had exported it. <code>rocx dev</code> names
					the <code>.env</code> in its folder. Without the flag no file is read, so test, CI and production
					servers have only the environment they were given. A variable already in the environment keeps
					its value. Lines are <code>NAME=value</code> (or <code>export NAME=value</code>), with
					<code>#</code> comments and quoted values; any other line stops the server, saying which. The log
					names what it set, never a value. Keep the file out of git.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Env.public_url!({"{}"})</code> is where the app is, for a link in an email or another
					server's callback: <code>--public-url</code> (or <code>PUBLIC_URL</code>) if given, such as the
					proxy in front (<code>https://status.example.com</code>). Otherwise, where the server listens, with
					the port it bound (even one picked for <code>--port 0</code>). Never a request's <code>Host</code>
					header, which anyone can write.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The platform's own settings are flags, each with an environment variable: <code>DATABASE_URL</code>,
				<code>PORT</code>, <code>MAX_BODY</code> and the rest (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#running"}>running
				it</a>). A package's settings are read by the app or its plugin: <code>STORAGE_URL</code> for
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_storage}>the storage package</a>, <code>SMTP_URL</code> for
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_email}>email</a>. A plugin that needs a variable says so, and the server
				won't start without it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="context">Read once: the app's context</Heading>
			<p>
				Read a setting every request needs, and that never changes, once. The app's <code>init!</code> runs
				at start (after the migrations, so it may read the database) and makes the app's
				<strong>context</strong>. The host gives it to every request's middleware, actor turn and plugin
				call. If <code>init!</code> returns an <code>Err</code>, the server stops with that message:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"program = App.program({\n\tinit!: |_started|\n\t\tmatch Env.var!(\"APP_SECRET\") {\n\t\t\tOk(secret) => Ok({ secret })\n\t\t\tErr(NotFound) => Err(\"APP_SECRET isn't set\")\n\t\t},\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>init!</code> gets the text of the <code>--context-file</code> (empty without one): a way to
				hand the app facts at start that aren't environment variables. This site passes its build's facts
				that way. How middleware hands the context to pages and endpoints is on the
				<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>routing</a> page.
			</p>

			<Heading id="dev">Only while developing</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Env.dev!({"{}"})</code> says whether the server runs with <code>--dev</code>, as
				<code>rocx dev</code> and <code>just dev</code> start it. Use it for what only a developer's machine
				should do, like the "sign in as anyone" shortcut above. Under <code>--dev</code> the server also
				reloads open pages on restart, shows a crash's reason in the answer, and, with the email plugin and
				no <code>SMTP_URL</code>, keeps outgoing email at <code>/__rocx/mail</code>
				(<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_email}#mailbox"}>the dev mailbox</a>). <code>Env.debug!({"{}"})</code>
				says whether it runs with <code>--debug</code>, as tests start it; the email plugin keeps mail for
				the tests then too. With neither flag, as deployed, a server with the email plugin and no
				<code>SMTP_URL</code> won't start rather than lose email; nor will one whose app or plugins have
				tables but no database.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
